  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nThe low pressure system over California will continue to move inland \nand lift northward today, then overhead on Friday before exiting the \nregion on Saturday. Showers and thunderstorms will overspread the \nCWA late this afternoon and tonight into Friday morning. \nPrecipitation will trend lighter moving into Friday evening and \nconclude for the entirety of the forecast area by early Saturday \nmorning.\n\nThe 48-hour National Blend mean QPF has increased slightly, sitting \nat roughly an inch across much of the forecast area, with closer to \n1.25 to 1.50 inches across the central mountains eastward into the \nIsland Park region. The 10th percentile is around 0.25\" to 0.50\" for \nmuch of eastern Idaho and closer to 0.75\" for the central mountains \ninto the Island Park region. The 90th percentile has increased to \n1.50 to 2 inches for most of east Idaho, with around 2 inches for \nthe central mountains. The official WPC forecast continues to run on \nthe dry end, still sitting between the 10th and 25th percentile with \nmuch of the area forecast to receive less than 0.75\" with 1 inch to \n1.25 inches across the high elevations of the central mountains and \nMontana Divide region. Thus, a large spread continues to reflect a \nbroad range of model solutions. \n\nThe snowfall forecast remains consistent with most of the snow \nfalling above 7000 feet and mainly across the central mountains. \nThere will be a 20 degree or so drop in temps between Thursday and \nFriday and temperatures Saturday morning will start off below \nfreezing for many, but precipitation will largely be concluded by \nthat time. \n\nDry weather is likely over the weekend into early next week. \nTemperatures will be a touch below average over the weekend and \nreturn to seasonable levels for next week.",
